For every $0<\delta<1/20$, there are vectors $X_N=(X_{N,0},\ldots,X_{N,N-1})\in[-1,1]^N$, one for each integer $N\ge1$, such that $$\begin{aligned}
 \limsup_{N\to\infty}\frac{\left\lVert Q_{X_N}\right\rVert_\infty}{\sqrt N}
 &\le K_\delta,
 &K_\delta&=\sqrt{\frac{(1+\delta)^3}{1-\delta}},
 \label{eq:almost-maximum}\\
 \liminf_{N\to\infty}\frac1N\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}X_{N,k}^2
 &\ge1-7\delta.\label{eq:almost-energy}
\end{aligned}$$

There is an absolute constant $C$ such that for every $N\ge1$ and every $X\in[-1,1]^N$, some signs $\varepsilon_0,\ldots,\varepsilon_{N-1}$ satisfy $$\label{eq:defect-rounding}
 \max_{t\in\mathbb T}\left|\sum_{k=0}^{N-1}(\varepsilon_k-X_k)\mathrm e(kt)\right|
 \le C\left(1+\sqrt{\mu(X)\log\frac{80N}{\mu(X)}}\right).$$ The square-root term is defined to be zero when $\mu(X)=0$; in that case $X$ already has sign entries and the left side may be zero.

For every $0<\delta<1/20$, there exist integers $m\ge1$ and $r\ge2$, nonzero vectors $a_1,\ldots,a_r\in\mathbb Z^m$, a real-valued trigonometric polynomial $F$ on $\mathbb T^m$, and a vector $v\in\mathbb R^m$ with the following properties. No two of the vectors $a_i$ are parallel, and $$\mathcal A=\{a_1,-a_1,\ldots,a_r,-a_r\}$$ is a containing Fourier support of $F$. Moreover, $$\label{eq:auxiliary-norms}
 \left\lVert F\right\rVert_\infty\le1,
 \qquad \left\lVert F\right\rVert_2\ge1-3\delta.$$ Writing $\lambda_a=a\cdot v$ for $a\in\mathcal A$, we have $$\label{eq:auxiliary-widths}
 \lambda_a\ne0,
 \qquad \sum_{a\in\mathcal A}|\lambda_a|<1,
 \qquad
 \frac{|\widehat F(a)|}{\sqrt{|\lambda_a|}}
 \le K_\delta,
 \qquad
 K_\delta=\sqrt{\frac{(1+\delta)^3}{1-\delta}}.$$ All these objects depend on $\delta$ alone.

Let $g\in C_c^\infty(\mathbb R)$ and let $\beta\in\mathbb R\setminus\{0\}$ be fixed. As $T\to\infty$ through positive real values, $$\label{eq:uniform-quadratic}
 \begin{split}
 \int_{\mathbb R}g(x)\mathrm e(T\beta x^2/2-ux)\,dx
 ={}&\frac{\mathrm e(\operatorname{sgn}(\beta)/8-u^2/(2T\beta))}
 {\sqrt{T|\beta|}}\\
 &\qquad\cdot\left(g\left(\frac{u}{T\beta}\right)
       +O_{g,\beta}(T^{-1})\right),
 \end{split}$$ uniformly for every real $u$.
